    "output_tail": "Let me look at the environment first.\nThis is a big design task: a chess move generator using only sequential regex substitutions. Let me check that python-chess is available and think through the design.\nVery close: the move count is correct (20); only the compression is broken. A single dot isn't converted to `1` (I only looped down to 2). Fix by adding `\\.` \u2192 `1`.\nThe exact-string comparison is `False`; let me see why (likely move ordering).\n`/app/re.json` is written: 4,806 `[regex, replacement]` pairs, 1.2 MB, well under both limits. The provided `check.py` passes, and so did about 4,000 further positions I compared against python-chess.\n\nYour example position gives the same three positions, but in a different order (king on g2, then king on e2, then queen on d1). `check.py` compares sets, so order doesn't matter there. If your scorer compares the exact string, the order would need fixing.\n\nThe checks I ran:\n- **Random games:** 1,024 white-to-move positions from random play, no mismatches.\n- **Targeted positions:** about 2,900 generated positions focused on castling, en passant, promotion and pins, no mismatches.\n- **Hand-built edge cases:**\n  - Castling through or out of check, and losing rights when a rook is captured or moves.\n  - A pinned en-passant pawn, and en passant exposing the king along the rank.\n  - Capture promotions.\n  - The standard perft test positions.\n\n**No legal moves:** if the position has no legal moves (checkmate or stalemate), the function returns `['']` instead of an empty list. This comes from `\"\".split(\"\\n\")` in the harness and can't be fixed in the regexes. `check.py` never feeds such a position, so I left it.\n\n**En-passant field:** the output en-passant square appears only when black has a legal en-passant capture, matching what python-chess prints.\n\nThe generator is `/app/gen/gen.py`; rerunning it rewrites `re.json`. The test scripts (`fuzz.py`, `fuzz2.py`, `edge.py`) are in `/app/gen` too.",
